ine \\( \\theta_k = \\frac{2k\\pi}{n+1} \\) for \\( k = 0, 1, 2, \\ldots, n \\).\n\n2. Consider the sum of cosine terms for any \\( m = 1, 2, \\ldots, n \\):\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{k=0}^{n} \\cos(m \\theta_k) = 0\n   \\]\n   and similarly,\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{k=0}^{n} \\sin(m \\theta_k) = 0.\n   \\]\n\n3. To understand why these sums are zero, use the identity involving the exponential form. Specifically,\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{k=0}^{n} e^{im\\theta_k}.\n   \\]\n   Remember that \\( \\theta_k = \\frac{2k\\pi}{n+1} \\), so:\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{k=0}^{n} e^{i m \\theta_k} = \\sum_{k=0}^{n} e^{i m \\frac{2k\\pi}{n+1}}.\n   \\]\n\n4. This sum is a geometric series with the common ratio \\( e^{\\frac{i 2m\\pi}{n+1}} \\). The sum of this geometric series can be written as:\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{k=0}^{n} e^{i m \\theta_k} = \\sum_{k=0}^{n} \\left(e^{\\frac{i 2m\\pi}{n+1}}\\right)^k = \\frac{1 - \\left(e^{\\frac{i 2m\\pi}{n+1}}\\right)^{n+1}}{1 - e^{\\frac{i 2m\\pi}{n+1}}}.\n   \\]\n\n5. Notice that:\n   \\[\n   \\left(e^{\\frac{i 2m\\pi}{n+1}}\\right)^{n+1} = e^{i 2m\\pi} = 1.\n   \\]\n\n6. Therefore, the sum becomes:\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{k=0}^{n} e^{i m \\theta_k} = \\frac{1 - 1}{1 - e^{\\frac{i 2m\\pi}{n+1}}} = 0.\n   \\]\n\n7. By separating real and imaginary parts, we get:\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{k=0}^{n} \\cos(m \\theta_k) = 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\sum_{k=0}^{n} \\sin(m \\theta_k) = 0 \\quad \\text{for} \\quad m = 1, 2, \\ldots, n.\n   \\]\n\n8. Use this result in the given function \\( g(\\theta) \\):\n   \\[\n   g(\\theta) = \\lambda_1 \\cos \\theta + \\lambda_2 \\cos 2\\theta + \\cdots + \\lambda_n \\cos n\\theta.\n   \\]\n\n9. Consider the sum of \\( g(\\theta_k) \\) over all \\( k \\):\n   \\[\n   g(0) + g(\\theta_1) + g(\\theta_2) + \\cdots + g(\\theta_n).\n   \\]\n\n10. Rewrite the sum using \\( \\lambda_1, \\lambda_2, \\ldots, \\lambda_n \\):\n    \\[\n    g(0) + g(\\theta_1) + g(\\theta_2) + \\cdots + g(\\theta_n) = \n    \\lambda_1 \\left( \\cos 0 + \\cos \\theta_1 + \\cos