ence, the answer is no.\n\nBut let me think again. Maybe there's another way to assign the sides. Perhaps the third side, which is 25% of the perimeter, is the longest side? Wait, but the problem says the shortest side is 20% of the longest side. So the longest side is separate from the third side. Wait, but if the third side is 25% of the perimeter, perhaps depending on the perimeter, maybe the third side is actually the longest. Let me check.\n\nSuppose the third side (which is 25% of the perimeter) is the longest. Then, in that case, the longest side is M = 0.25P. Then the shortest side is 20% of the longest side, so 0.2M.\n\nBut then the third side would be the remaining side. Wait, this is confusing. Let me clarify.\n\nWait, maybe I misassigned the sides. Let me define all sides properly.\n\nLet me denote:\n\nLet the sides be a, b, c, where a < b < c. So c is the longest, a is the shortest.\n\nGiven that a = 0.2c (shortest is 20% of the longest).\n\nThe third side, which is b, is 25% of the perimeter. So b = 0.25P, where P = a + b + c.\n\nSo substituting a = 0.2c and b = 0.25P into P = a + b + c:\n\nP = 0.2c + 0.25P + c\n\nCombine like terms:\n\nP - 0.25P = 0.2c + c\n\n0.75P = 1.2c\n\nThus, P = (1.2 / 0.75)c = 1.6c\n\nTherefore, b = 0.25 * 1.6c = 0.4c\n\nSo the sides are a = 0.2c, b = 0.4c, c = c. So ordered as 0.2c, 0.4c, c. Then check triangle inequalities:\n\n1. a + b > c => 0.2c + 0.4c = 0.6c > c? 0.6c > c => 0.6 > 1? False.\n\n2. a + c > b => 0.2c + c = 1.2c > 0.4c? 1.2c > 0.4c, which is true.\n\n3. b + c > a => 0.4c + c = 1.4c > 0.2c, which is true.\n\nBut the first inequality fails. Therefore, such a triangle cannot exist.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the third side (which is 25% of perimeter) is actually the longest side. Let's explore that possibility.\n\nSuppose the third side is the longest. Let me re-examine.\n\nSuppose the sides are a, b, c with a < b < c.\n\nGiven that a = 0.2c (shortest is 20% of longest), and one of the sides is 25% of the perimete