ng. The key step was recognizing that in each triangle, the distance from the vertex to the touch point can be calculated using the formula (sum of adjacent sides - opposite side)/2. Then, by computing CM and CN, subtracting them gives MN. But in the example, it worked out. The variable h canceled out, which is interesting because the height of the triangle didn't affect the result. That suggests that MN only depends on AE and BE, not on the specific dimensions of the triangle ABC. Which seems counter-intuitive at first, but the math checks out.\n\nTherefore, the answer should be MN = |AE - BE| / 2. Since the problem says \"given the lengths of the segments AE and BE\", we can present the answer as half the absolute difference between AE and BE.\n\nBut let me check another example. Suppose AE = 5, BE = 3, so AB = 8. Then MN should be |5 - 3| / 2 = 1. Let's verify.\n\nUsing coordinates again. Let ABC be isosceles with AB = 8, so A(0,0), B(8,0), C(4, h). Point E is at (5,0). CE is from (4, h) to (5, 0). Compute incircle touch points.\n\nFor triangle ACE: sides AC = sqrt(16 + h\u00b2 ), AE = 5, CE = sqrt(1 + h\u00b2 )\n\nCM = (AC + CE - AE)/2 = (sqrt(16 + h\u00b2 ) + sqrt(1 + h\u00b2 ) - 5)/2\n\nFor triangle ECB: sides EC = sqrt(1 + h\u00b2 ), EB = 3, BC = sqrt(16 + h\u00b2 )\n\nEN = (EB + EC - BC)/2 = (3 + sqrt(1 + h\u00b2 ) - sqrt(16 + h\u00b2 )) /2\n\nCN = CE - EN = sqrt(1 + h\u00b2 ) - [ (3 + sqrt(1 + h\u00b2 ) - sqrt(16 + h\u00b2 )) /2 ] = [ 2 sqrt(1 + h\u00b2 ) - 3 - sqrt(1 + h\u00b2 ) + sqrt(16 + h\u00b2 ) ] / 2 = [ sqrt(1 + h\u00b2 ) -3 + sqrt(16 + h\u00b2 ) ] /2\n\nThen MN = |CM - CN| = | [ (sqrt(16 + h\u00b2 ) + sqrt(1 + h\u00b2 ) -5)/2 - (sqrt(1 + h\u00b2 ) -3 + sqrt(16 + h\u00b2 )) /2 ] | = | [ sqrt(16 + h\u00b2 ) + sqrt(1 + h\u00b2 ) -5 - sqrt(1 + h\u00b2 ) +3 - sqrt(16 + h\u00b2 ) ] /2 | = | (-2)/2 | = 1. Which is |5 - 3| /2 = 1. Correct again.\n\nTherefore, the formula holds. Thus, the length MN is half the absolute difference between AE and BE. Therefor