\\) such that \\(AC = BC\\).\n2. A point \\(E\\) is taken on the base \\(AB\\).\n3. Circles are inscribed in the triangles \\(ACE\\) and \\(ECB\\) that touch the segment \\(CE\\) at points \\(M\\) and \\(N\\), respectively.\n4. According to the problem, the lengths \\(AE\\) and \\(BE\\) are known.\n5. Calculate the length \\(CM\\) where the circle inscribed in \\(\\triangle ACE\\) touches \\(CE\\).\n   - Using the segment formula for inscribed circles, it is given by:\n     \\[\n     CM = \\frac{AC + CE - AE}{2}\n     \\]\n6. Similarly, calculate \\(CN\\) where the circle inscribed in \\(\\triangle ECB\\) touches \\(CE\\).\n   - This length is given by:\n     \\[\n     CN = \\frac{BC + CE - BE}{2}\n     \\]\n7. Given that \\(AC = BC\\), the above expressions can be simplified. Both \\(AC\\) and \\(BC\\) are the same, simplifying as:\n   \\[\n   CM = \\frac{AC + CE - AE}{2}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   CN = \\frac{BC + CE - BE}{2} = \\frac{AC + CE - BE}{2}\n   \\]\n8. The length \\(MN\\) (the distance between \\(M\\) and \\(N\\)) is the absolute difference between \\(CM\\) and \\(CN\\):\n   \\[\n   MN = |CM - CN|\n   \\]\n9. Substitute the expressions for \\(CM\\) and \\(CN\\):\n   \\[\n   MN = \\left| \\frac{AC + CE - AE}{2} - \\frac{AC + CE - BE}{2} \\right|\n   \\]\n10. Simplify the expression inside the absolute value:\n    \\[\n    MN = \\left| \\frac{(AC + CE - AE) - (AC + CE - BE)}{2} \\right|\n    \\]\n    \\[\n    MN = \\left| \\frac{AC + CE - AE - AC - CE + BE}{2} \\right|\n    \\]\n    \\[\n    MN = \\left| \\frac{BE - AE}{2} \\right|\n    \\]\n11. Finally, the length \\(MN\\) is given by:\n    \\[\n    MN = \\frac{|BE - AE|}{2}\n    \\]\n\n### Conclusion\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{|BE - AE|}{2}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "\nIn the following table containing logarithms, two values are incorrect. Please correct them.\n\n\\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|}\n\\hline\n$x$ & 0.021 & 0.27 & 1.5 & 2.8 