\) are concyclic.\n\n### Conclusion:\nBy reflecting \\(J\\) over \\(BC\\) and using the properties of the excenter and the right angles created by \\(E\\) and \\(F\\), we can conclude that the points \\(F, K, E, J\\) lie on a circle. This concyclicity implies that the sum of the angles \\(\\angle FKE\\) and \\(\\angle FJE\\) is \\(180^\\circ\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{180^\\circ}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Introduce Key Elements and Definitions:**\n   Let \\( ABC \\) be a triangle and \\( J \\) be the center of the \\( A \\)-excircle. The reflection of \\( J \\) in the line \\( BC \\) is \\( K \\). Points \\( E \\) and \\( F \\) lie on \\( BJ \\) and \\( CJ \\), respectively, such that \\( \\angle EAB = \\angle CAF = 90^\\circ \\). We need to prove that \\( \\angle FKE + \\angle FJE = 180^\\circ \\).\n\n2. **Identify Intersection Point and Initial Angles:**\n   Let \\( JK \\) intersect \\( BC \\) at \\( X \\). Notice that \\( \\angle EAB = 90^\\circ = \\angle KXB \\). Also, \\( BJ \\) bisects \\( \\angle CBA \\), thus since \\( J \\) is the \\( A \\)-excenter, \\( \\angle JBX = \\frac{1}{2}\\angle CBA \\).\n\n3. **Prove Similarity of Triangles:**\n   - Triangles \\( EBA \\) and \\( KXB \\) are similar:\n     \\[ \\angle EBA = \\angle JBX = \\angle KBX \\]\n     \\[ \\angle EAB = 90^\\circ = \\angle KXB \\]\n     Therefore, \\( \\triangle EBA \\sim \\triangle KBX \\).\n   \n   - Similarly, \\( \\triangle CFA \\) and \\( \\triangle KXC \\) are similar:\n     \\[ \\angle CAF = 90^\\circ = \\angle KXC \\]\n     \\[ \\angle FAC = \\angle JXC = \\angle KXC \\]\n     Therefore, \\( \\triangle CFA \\sim \\triangle KXC \\).\n\n4. **Utilize Symmetry and Similarity:**\n   Since \\( J \\) and \\( K \\) are symmetric with respect to \\( BC \\), the segment \\( EK \\) is reflected to \\( FJ \\), and the triangles' correspondence ensures that:\n   \\[ \\angle FKE + \\angle BKC = 360^\\circ - \\angle EKB - \\angle CKA \\]\n   \n5. **Calculate and Substitute Angles:**\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\angle FKE + \\angle FJE &= \\angle FKE + \\angle BKF \\quad (\\text{since } K \\text{ and } J \\text{ are reflections}) \\\\\n   &= 360^\\circ - \\angle EKB - \\angle CKF \\\\\n   &= 360^\\circ - \\angle AXB - \\angle CXA \\quad (\\text{due to similarity relations}) \\\\\n   &= 360^\\circ - 180^\\circ \\quad (\\text{since } \\angle AXB \\text{ and } \\angle CXA \\text{ form a full circle}) \\\\\n   &= 180^\\circ\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Conclusion:**\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{180^\\circ}\n   \\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "On a beautiful Sunday spring morning, the father of a family went for a walk with his sons.\n\n- Have you noticed, - he said to them, - that the age of the oldest of you is equal to the sum of the ages of your two other brothers?\n- Yes. And we also noticed - they answered in unison - that the product of our ages and your age equals the sum of the cube of the number of your sons, multiplied by a thousand, and ten times the square of this number.\n\nCan you determine from this conversation the age of the father at the time of the birth of the second son?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let me try to figure out this problem step by step. So, the father is talking to his sons on a spring morning, and there's some information about their ages. The goal is to find the father's age when the second son was born. Hmm, alright, let's parse the problem carefully.\n\nFirst, the father says that the age of the oldest son is equal to the sum of the ages of the two other brothers. So, there are three sons, right? The oldest, middle, and youngest. Let's denote their current ages as O, M, and Y. Then, according to the father, O = M + Y. Got that.\n\nThen the sons respond with another statement. They mention that the product of their ages and the father's age equals the sum of the cube of the number of sons multiplied by a thousand, plus ten times the square of t