 points. Therefore, such a non-acute triangle must exist.", "deepseek_solution": "To prove that among four non-collinear points on a plane, there exists a non-acute triangle, we analyze two cases based on the convex hull of the points:\n\n**Case 1: Convex Hull is a Quadrilateral**  \nIf the four points form a convex quadrilateral, the sum of its interior angles is \\(360^\\circ\\). If all angles were acute (\\(<90^\\circ\\)), their total would be less than \\(4 \\times 90^\\circ = 360^\\circ\\), which is impossible. Hence, at least one angle must be \\(\\geq 90^\\circ\\). This angle belongs to a triangle formed by three consecutive vertices of the quadrilateral (e.g., angle \\(B\\) in quadrilateral \\(ABCD\\) is part of triangle \\(ABC\\)). Therefore, this triangle is non-acute.\n\n**Case 2: Convex Hull is a Triangle with One Point Inside**  \nIf three points form a triangle \\(ABC\\) and the fourth point \\(D\\) lies inside, connecting \\(D\\) to \\(A\\), \\(B\\), and \\(C\\) creates three smaller triangles (\\(ABD\\), \\(BCD\\), \\(ACD\\)). The angles at \\(D\\) in these triangles sum to \\(360^\\circ\\). By the pigeonhole principle, at least one angle at \\(D\\) must be \\(\\geq 120^\\circ\\), making the corresponding triangle non-acute.\n\n**Conclusion**  \nIn both configurations, a non-acute triangle must exist. Therefore, any four non-collinear points guarantee the existence of at least one non-acute triangle.\n\n**Final Answer**  \nAmong any four non-collinear points on a plane, there necessarily exists a triangle with a non-acute angle. This is proven by analyzing the convex hull: if it forms a quadrilateral, one angle must be \u226590\u00b0, and if it forms a triangle with an interior point, an angle at the interior point must be \u2265120\u00b0. Thus, \\boxed{\\text{Such a non-acute triangle always exists}}.", "ground_truth_solution": ": \n\nLet us examine various configurations of the four given points on the plane to demonstrate the existence of a non-acute triangle formed by these points.\n\n1. If the four points are vertices of a convex quadrilateral, then the sum of the interior angles is \\(360^\\circ\\). \n   \n   Consider a convex quadrilateral \\(ABCD\\). The sum of the interior angles of \\(ABCD\\) can be expressed as:\n   \\[\n   \\angle A + \\angle B + \\angle C + \\angle D = 360^\\circ\n   \\]\n   \n   Since none of the angles can individually exceed \\(180^\\circ\\), it follows that at least one of these angles must be non-acute (i.e., \\(\\geq 90^\\circ\\)). Let us assume \\(\\angle D\\) is non-acute.\n   \n   Form a triangle using vertex \\(D\\) and its two adjacent vertices:\n   \\[\n   \\triangle ABD \\quad \\text{or} \\quad \\triangle BCD\n   \\]\n   Since \\(\\angle D\\) is non-acute, either \\(\\triangle ABD\\) or \\(\\triangle BCD\\) is non-acute.\n   \n   Thus, in the case of a convex quadrilateral:\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\text{Existence of a non-acute triangle proven}}\n   \\]\n\n2. If one point lies inside the triangle formed by the other three points, consider the four points \\(A, B, C, D\\) where point \\(A\\) lies inside \\(\\triangle BCD\\). \n\n   Since point \\(A\\) is inside \\(\\triangle BCD\\), the angles formed at \\(A\\) with the vertices \\(B, C, D\\) can be added up:\n   \\[\n   \\angle BAC + \\angle CAD + \\angle DAB = 360^\\circ\n   \\]\n   \n   Again, since the sum must be \\(360^\\circ\\), at least one of \\(\\angle BAC\\), \\(\\angle CAD\\), or \\(\\angle DAB\\) must be \\(\\geq 120^\\circ\\).\n   \n   Suppose \\(\\angle CAD \\geq 120^\\circ\\), form the triangle:\n   \\[\n   \\triangle CAD\n   \\]\n   This triangle is non-acute because it contains \\(\\angle CAD\\) which is \\(\\geq 120^\\circ\\).\n   \n   Thus, in the case where one point is inside a triangle:\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\text{Existence of a non-acute triangle proven}}\n   \\]\n\n3. If three points are collinear, label the points \\(A, B, C\\) such that \\(A, B, C\\) are on the same line in that order, and let the