=j + 1}^n 1/k is less than or equal to sum_{k=j + 1}^\\infty 1/k, which diverges, but for finite n, it's manageable. However, perhaps integrating to approximate the sum.\n\nRecall that for m \u2265 1, H_m \u2248 ln m + \u03b3, where \u03b3 is Euler-Mascheroni constant, but maybe we can use inequalities. For the harmonic series, we have:\n\nln(n + 1) \u2264 H_n \u2264 1 + ln n\n\nBut maybe integrating:\n\nsum_{k = a}^b 1/k \u2264 \u222b_{a - 1}^b 1/x dx = ln(b) - ln(a - 1)\n\nSimilarly, sum_{k = a}^b 1/k \u2265 \u222b_{a}^{b + 1} 1/x dx = ln(b + 1) - ln(a)\n\nBut perhaps this is overcomplicating. Let me instead think about the term 1 - sum_{k = j + 1}^n 1/k. Let's compute sum_{k = j + 1}^n 1/k for j from 1 to n - 1. For example, if j = n - 1, then sum_{k = n}^n 1/k = 1/n, so 1 - 1/n = (n - 1)/n. If j = 1, then sum_{k=2}^n 1/k = H_n - 1, so 1 - (H_n - 1) = 2 - H_n. Hmm, but how does this help?\n\nAlternatively, maybe consider that sum_{k=j + 1}^n 1/k = H_n - H_j. So 1 - (H_n - H_j) = 1 + H_j - H_n. Hmm. But I need to bound |1 + H_j - H_n|. For j from 1 to n - 1.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can bound this expression using integrals. Let me recall that H_n = 1 + 1/2 + ... + 1/n. The integral from 1 to n + 1 of 1/x dx = ln(n + 1) \u2264 H_n \u2264 1 + ln n.\n\nBut perhaps more precise bounds. Let me think.\n\nAlternatively, since we have |f(n) - S(n)| \u2264 sum_{j=1}^{n - 1} j |1 - sum_{k=j + 1}^n 1/k|, and we need to show that this is \u2264 n(n - 1)/4.\n\nSo perhaps we can find that for each j, |1 - sum_{k=j + 1}^n 1/k| \u2264 (n - 1 - j)/4 or something like that. Wait, but this is just a guess.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can compute the maximum value of the expression sum_{j=1}^{n - 1} j |1 - sum_{k=j + 1}^n 1/k| and see if it's bounded by n(n - 1)/4.\n\nBut this seems complicated. Maybe there's another approach. Let me try to think differently.\n\nThe given condition is |f(m) + f(n) - f(m + n)| \u2264 m n. Maybe if I set m = n, then |2 f(n) - f(2n)| \u2264 n^2. Simil