n:\n     \\[\n     f(n, r) = (2n - r) f(n-1, r-1) + f(n-1, r)\n     \\]\n\n3. **Verification of the Solution:**\n   - Given solution \\( f(n, r) = \\binom{n}{r} \\frac{n!}{(n-r)!} \\) fits the recurrence relation:\n\n     - Base case is verified as:\n       \\[\n       \\binom{n}{0} \\frac{n!}{n!} = 1\n       \\]\n\n     - Applying it to the recurrence:\n       \\[\n       \\begin{aligned}\n       f(n, r) &= (2n - r) f(n-1, r-1) + f(n-1, r)\\\\\n       &= (2n - r) \\binom{n-1}{r-1} \\frac{(n-1)!}{(n-r)!} + \\binom{n-1}{r} \\frac{(n-1)!}{(n-r-1)!}\\\\\n       &= (2n - r) \\times \\frac{(n-1)!}{(r-1)! (n-r)!} \\frac{(n-1)!}{(n-r)!} + \\frac{(n-1)!}{r! (n-r-1)!} \\frac{(n-1)!}{(n-r-1)!}\\\\\n       &= \\binom{n}{r} \\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}\n       \\end{aligned}\n       \\]\n\n- Valid since the results match the state relation and boundary conditions.\n\nConclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\binom{n}{r} \\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "For which \\( n \\) will \\( \\binom{n}{k} \\) be odd for every \\( 0 \\leq k \\leq n \\)?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to figure out for which values of \\( n \\) the binomial coefficient \\( \\binom{n}{k} \\) is odd for every \\( k \\) from 0 to \\( n \\). Hmm, interesting problem. Let me start by recalling some properties of binomial coefficients and their parity.\n\nFirst, I remember Lucas' Theorem, which relates the binomial coefficients modulo a prime number (in this case, 2) to the digits of \\( n \\) and \\( k \\) in base 2. Lucas' Theorem states that for non-negative integers \\( n \\) and \\( k \\), and a prime \\( p \\), the binomial coefficient \\( \\binom{n}{k} \\) modulo \\( p \\) can be determined by looking at the base-\\( p \\) expansions of \\( n \\) and \\( k \\). Specifically, if \\( n = n_m p^m + \\dots + n_0 \\) and \\( k = k_m p^m + \\dots + k_0 \\), then:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{n}{k} \\equiv \\prod_{i=0}^m \\binom{n_i}{k_i} \\mod p\n