t testing with the smallest values of \\( a \\).\n\n**Case \\( a = 1 \\):**\n\nWe need \\( b \\) and \\( c \\) such that:\n\n- \\( b^2 > 4c \\)\n- \\( 0 < b < 2 \\) (since \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b < 2 \\times 1 = 2 \\))\n- \\( 1 - b + c > 0 \\)\n- \\( 0 < b < 2 \\)\n\nBut \\( b \\) is an integer, so \\( b = 1 \\).\n\nPlugging \\( b = 1 \\):\n\n- \\( 1^2 > 4c \\implies 1 > 4c \\implies c < 1/4 \\). But \\( c \\) must be a positive integer, so \\( c \\geq 1 \\). Thus, no solution here. Hence, \\( a = 1 \\) is impossible.\n\n**Case \\( a = 2 \\):**\n\nConditions:\n\n- \\( b^2 > 8c \\)\n- \\( 0 < b < 4 \\implies b = 1, 2, 3 \\)\n- \\( 2 - b + c > 0 \\implies c > b - 2 \\)\n\nLet's check each possible \\( b \\):\n\n1. \\( b = 1 \\):\n   - \\( 1^2 > 8c \\implies 1 > 8c \\implies c < 1/8 \\). Not possible (c \u2265 1).\n2. \\( b = 2 \\):\n   - \\( 4 > 8c \\implies c < 0.5 \\). Not possible.\n3. \\( b = 3 \\):\n   - \\( 9 > 8c \\implies c < 9/8 \u2248 1.125 \\implies c = 1 \\)\n   - Check \\( 2 - 3 + 1 = 0 \\). But we need \\( 2 - 3 + c > 0 \\implies c > 1 \\). But \\( c = 1 \\) gives equality. Hence, no solution here.\n\nThus, \\( a = 2 \\) also impossible.\n\n**Case \\( a = 3 \\):**\n\nConditions:\n\n- \\( b^2 > 12c \\)\n- \\( 0 < b < 6 \\implies b = 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 \\)\n- \\( 3 - b + c > 0 \\implies c > b - 3 \\)\n\nCheck each \\( b \\):\n\n1. \\( b = 1 \\):\n   - \\( 1 > 12c \\implies c < 1/12 \\). Impossible.\n2. \\( b = 2 \\):\n   - \\( 4 > 12c \\implies c < 1/3 \\). Impossible.\n3. \\( b = 3 \\):\n   - \\( 9 > 12c \\implies c < 0.75 \\). Impossible.\n4. \\( b = 4 \\):\n   - \\( 16 > 12c \\implies c < 16/12 \u2248 1.333 \\implies c = 1 \\)\n   - Check \\( 3 - 4 + 1 = 0 \\). Again, need \\( c > 1 \\), but c=1 gives 0. So no.\n5. \\( b = 5 \\):\n   - \\( 25 > 12c \\implies c < 25/12 \u2248 2.083 \\implies c = 1, 2 \\)\n   - For c=1: \\( 3 -5 +1 = -1 <0 \\)\n   - For c=2: \\( 3 -5 +2 =0 \\). Still not greater than 0. So no.\n\nThus, \\( a=3 \\) impossible.\n\n**Case \\( a = 4 