{13}\\) are \\(x \\equiv 1, 3, 9 \\pmod{13}\\).\n\nNext, we express the number \\(\\overline{2a1b9}\\) in terms of its digits and compute its value modulo 13. The number can be written as \\(N = 20000 + 1000a + 100 + 10b + 9\\). Using modular arithmetic for powers of 10 modulo 13, we find:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n10^4 &\\equiv 3 \\pmod{13}, \\\\\n10^3 &\\equiv 12 \\pmod{13}, \\\\\n10^2 &\\equiv 9 \\pmod{13}, \\\\\n10^1 &\\equiv 10 \\pmod{13}, \\\\\n10^0 &\\equiv 1 \\pmod{13}.\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nThus, \\(N \\equiv 2 \\cdot 3 + a \\cdot 12 + 1 \\cdot 9 + b \\cdot 10 + 9 \\cdot 1 \\pmod{13}\\). Simplifying, we get:\n\n\\[\nN \\equiv 11 + 12a + 10b \\pmod{13}.\n\\]\n\nWe need \\(11 + 12a + 10b \\equiv 1, 3, 9 \\pmod{13}\\), which simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n12a + 10b \\equiv 3, 5, 11 \\pmod{13}.\n\\]\n\nRewriting \\(12 \\equiv -1 \\pmod{13}\\) and \\(10 \\equiv 10 \\pmod{13}\\), we get:\n\n\\[\n-a + 10b \\equiv c \\pmod{13} \\quad \\text{for} \\quad c \\in \\{3, 5, 11\\}.\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to \\(10b - a \\equiv c \\pmod{13}\\). For each \\(c \\in \\{3, 5, 11\\}\\), we check pairs \\((a, b)\\) where \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are digits (0-9). We compute \\(a \\equiv (10b - c) \\pmod{13}\\) and check if \\(a\\) is a valid digit.\n\nAfter checking all possibilities for each \\(c\\), we find the valid pairs:\n\n- For \\(c = 3\\): 7 pairs.\n- For \\(c = 5\\): 8 pairs.\n- For \\(c = 11\\): 8 pairs.\n\nSumming these, we get a total of \\(7 + 8 + 8 = 23\\) valid pairs.\n\nThus, the number of ordered digit pairs \\((a, b)\\) is \\(\\boxed{23}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. Let $\\overline{2 a 1 b 9} = m$. \n\n2. By Fermat's Little Theorem, since $13$ is a prime number, we know:\n   \\[\n   m^{12} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{13}.\n   \\]\n\n3. Therefore, \n   \\[\n   m^{2019} = m^{12 \\cdot 168 + 3} = (m^{12})^{168} \\cdot m^3 \\equiv 1^{168} \\cdot m^3 = m^3 \\pmod{13}.\n   \\]\n\n4. To solve $m^3 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{13}$, we need to check all possible residues of $m$ modulo $13$. \n\nW