L\\).\n   - Let \\( S_4 \\) be the area of triangle \\(DLM\\).\n   - Let \\( x \\) be the area of triangle \\(KMN\\).\n   - Let \\( y \\) be the area of triangle \\(KLM\\).\n\n4. **Establish Area Relationships:** \n   Recall that the triangles \\(KMA\\) and \\(ABM\\) have equal areas as \\(MK \\parallel AB\\) implies these triangles share identical heights from line \\(AD\\) and congruent bases. Thus, triangles \\(KMN\\) and \\(ABN\\) also have equal areas due to the same relative orientation and equal partition heights.\n\n5. **Ratios of Similar Triangles:**\n   We can use the similarity of the triangles involved to derive the following equations:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{x}{S_1} = \\frac{AN}{NK} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\frac{S_2}{x} = \\frac{AN}{NK}\n   \\]\n   This means:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{x}{S_1} = \\frac{S_2}{x} \\implies x = \\sqrt{S_1 S_2}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Additional Area Relationships:**\n   By analogous reasoning:\n   \\[\n   y = \\sqrt{S_3 S_4}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Applying the Arithmetic Mean-Geometric Mean (AM-GM) Inequality:**\n   Using the AM-GM inequality, one has:\n   \\[\n   S_1 + S_2 \\geq 2 \\sqrt{S_1 S_2} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad S_3 + S_4 \\geq 2 \\sqrt{S_3 S_4}\n   \\]\n\n8. **Summing Areas:**\n   The total area of the parallelogram excluding the quadrilateral \\(KLMN\\) is:\n   \\[\n   S_1 + S_2 + S_3 + S_4\n   \\]\n   Given that the total area of the parallelogram is \\(1\\), the area of \\(KLMN\\) can be represented as:\n   \\[\n   1 - (S_1 + S_2 + S_3 + S_4)\n   \\]\n\n9. **Maximizing the Ratio:**\n   Substituting the previously derived inequalities into the total area equation:\n   \\[\n   2(x + y) = 1 - (S_1 + S_2 + S_3 + S_4) \\leq 1 - 2(\\sqrt{S_1 S_2} + \\sqrt{S_3 S_4}) = 1 - 2(x + y)\n   \\]\n   This implies that:\n   \\[\n   x + y \\leq \\frac{1}{4}\n   \\]\n\n10. **Verification of Equality:**\n    The equality condition holds when \\(S_1 = S_2\\) and \\(S_3 = S_4\\), which occurs when \\(KM \\parallel AB\\).\n\n### Conclusion:\nThe maximum