fore, there are at least 2 students who solved both problems that S missed. Therefore, pairing S with any of these students would cover all problems. Hence, such a pair exists.\n\nSimilarly, if k=5, then there's 1 problem not solved by S, which has 8 solvers, so pairing S with any of them works.\n\nIf k=6, then S solved all problems, so pairing S with anyone else would trivially cover all problems.\n\nTherefore, this seems to be a valid proof. Let me summarize:\n\n1. Since each problem is solved by at least 8 students, the total number of problem solutions is at least 6*8=48.\n2. The average number of problems solved per student is 48/15=3.2. Therefore, by the pigeonhole principle, there exists at least one student who solved at least 4 problems.\n3. Let S be such a student who solved at least 4 problems. Let t be the number of problems S didn't solve, so t=6 -k \u22642.\n4. For each of these t problems, there are at least 8 solvers among the remaining 14 students.\n5. If t=1, then there's one problem not solved by S, which has at least 8 solvers. S and any solver of this problem will cover all 6 problems.\n6. If t=2, then consider the two problems not solved by S. Each has at least 8 solvers. The intersection of these two sets of solvers is at least 8 +8 -14=2 students. Therefore, there exist at least two students who solved both of these problems. Pairing S with any of these students results in covering all 6 problems.\n7. Therefore, in either case, there exists a pair of students who together solved all 6 problems.\n\nThus, the proof is complete.", "deepseek_solution": "To prove that there exist two students who, between them, solved all 6 problems, we proceed as follows:\n\n1. **Problem Constraints**: Each problem was solved by at least 8 students (more than half of 15). Thus, the total number of problem solutions is at least \\(6 \\times 8 = 48\\).\n\n2. **Average Solutions per Student**: The average number of problems solved per student is \\(\\frac{48}{15} = 3.2\\). By the pigeonhole principle, at least one student solved \\(\\lceil 3.2 \\rceil = 4\\) or more problems.\n\n3. **Case Analysis for Such a Student**:\n   - Let \\(S\\) be a student who solved \\(k \\geq 4\\) problems. They missed \\(t = 6 - k \\leq 2\\) problems.\n   - **If \\(t = 1\\)**: The one unsolved problem has at least 8 solvers. Pairing \\(S\\) with any of these solvers covers all 6 problems.\n   - **If \\(t = 2\\)**: Let the two unsolved problems be \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\). Each has at least 8 solvers. The intersection of these solvers (students who solved both \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\)) is at least \\(8 + 8 - 14 = 2\\) (by inclusion-exclusion). Pairing \\(S\\) with any student from this intersection covers all 6 problems.\n\n**Conclusion**: In all cases, there exists a pair of students who collectively solved all 6 problems. Hence, such a pair must exist. \n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{\\text{Such two students exist.}}", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. We begin by noting that there are 15 students in the class, and 6 geometry problems are assigned as homework.\n\n2. Given that each problem was solved by more than half of the students, this implies that each problem was solved by at least \\(8\\) students (since \\(\\lceil \\frac{15}{2} \\rceil = 8\\)).\n\n3. Consequently, the cumulative number of problem solutions by all students must be at least \\(8 \\times 6 = 48\\). Let\u2019s denote the total number of problem solutions by \\(S\\), hence:\n   \\[\n   S \\geq 48\n   \\]\n\n4. Averaging this total over the 15 students, we find the mean number of problems solved per student:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Average} = \\frac{S}{15} \\geq \\frac{48}{15} = 3 \\frac{1}{5} = 3.2\n   \\]\n\n5. This average indicates that there must be at least one student, say student \\(A\\), who solved at least \\(4\\) problems. Let\u2019s confirm this assumption:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Minimum number of problems solved by any student } \\geq 3.2\n   \\]\n   Hence, there is at least one student who solved \\(\\lceil 3.2 \\rceil = 4\\) problems.\n\n6. If one student solved all 6 problems, our proof is complete, as the requirement states finding two students who collectively solved all problems. In this case, this student and any other student provide a trivial solution:\n   \n   - Proof ends here if a student solved all 6 problems.\n\n7. Let us assume student \\(A\\) solved exactly \\(4\\) problems. There are then \\(15 - 1 = 14\\) other students who collectively are responsible for solving the remaining problems.\n\n8. The total number of solutions by others for the 2 unsolved problems must therefore be:\n   \\[\n   8 \\times 2 = 16 \\text{ solutions}\n   \\]\n   This count corresponds to the minimum number of solutions because each of the remaining \\(2\\) problems need to be solved at least \\(8\\) times.\n\n9. Since we assume there is a student, suspect \\(A\\), who has \\(4\\) solved problems, the remaining \\(14\\) students must solve these \\(2\\) problems at least:\n   \\[\n   16 \\text{ solutions among } 14 \\text{ students}\n   \\]\n\n10. By the pigeonhole principle, if each pigeonhole represents \\(1\\) of the remaining problems and each pigeon represents a solution by a student, there should be at least \\(1\\) student who solved both remaining problems. This fact negates the possibility of each remaining problem being solved exactly once by different \\(14\\) students.\n\n11. Therefore, there exists at least one student who solved exactly \\(5\\) problems if not more, alongside student \\(A\\), to meet the scenario. If any student solved fewer, the remaining solutions would still fit into previously handled cases via overlapping among \\(8\\).\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{There exist two students who collectively solved all 6 problems.}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Find the smallest \\( n \\) such that if \\( \\{1, 2, \\ldots, n\\} \\) is divided int