( x_n \\) is positive, this inequality may not hold. Wait, AM-GM applies to non-negative numbers. Since one is negative and one is positive, their product is negative, and the right-hand side is non-negative. So the inequality \\( x_1 x_n \\leq \\left( \\frac{x_1 + x_n}{2} \\right)^2 \\) would always hold because left side is negative and right side is non-negative. But this is trivial and not useful.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use the fact that \\( (x_1 - x_n)^2 \\geq0 \\implies x_1 x_n \\leq \\frac{x_1^2 + x_n^2}{2} \\). But again, since \\( x_1 x_n \\) is negative, and \\( x_1^2 +x_n^2 \\geq0 \\), this inequality is also trivial.\n\nAlternatively, consider the Lagrangian method without assuming the middle variables are equal. Let's set up the problem to maximize \\( x_1 x_n \\) subject to the constraints \\( \\sum x_i =0 \\) and \\( \\sum x_i^2 =1 \\). Let me use Lagrange multipliers with variables \\( x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n \\).\n\nThe function to maximize is \\( f(x_1, \\ldots, x_n) = x_1 x_n \\).\n\nThe constraints are:\n\n1. \\( g(x_1, \\ldots, x_n) = x_1 + x_2 + \\ldots + x_n =0 \\)\n2. \\( h(x_1, \\ldots, x_n) = x_1^2 + x_2^2 + \\ldots + x_n^2 -1 =0 \\)\n\nThe Lagrangian is:\n\n\\( \\mathcal{L} = x_1 x_n + \\lambda (x_1 + x_2 + \\ldots + x_n) + \\mu (x_1^2 + x_2^2 + \\ldots + x_n^2 -1) \\)\n\nTake partial derivatives with respect to each variable and set to zero.\n\nFor \\( x_1 \\):\n\n\\( \\frac{\\partial \\mathcal{L}}{\\partial x_1} = x_n + \\lambda + 2\\mu x_1 =0 \\)\n\nFor \\( x_n \\):\n\n\\( \\frac{\\partial \\mathcal{L}}{\\partial x_n} = x_1 + \\lambda + 2\\mu x_n =0 \\)\n\nFor \\( x_i \\), where \\( 2 \\leq i \\leq n-1 \\):\n\n\\( \\frac{\\partial \\mathcal{L}}{\\partial x_i} = 0 + \\lambda + 2\\mu x_i =0 \\)\n\nThus, we have the following equations:\n\n1. \\( x_n + \\lambda + 2\\mu x_1 =0 \\) --- (1)\n2. \\( x_1 + \\lambda + 2\\mu x_n =0 \\) --- (2)\n3. For each \\( 2 \\leq i \\leq n-1 \\): \\( \\lambda + 2\\mu x_i =0 \\) --- (3)\n4. Sum constraint: \\