eger } k\n   \\]\n\n6. Hence, there exist two integers \\( a_i \\) and \\( a_j \\) such that \\( a_i - a_j \\) is divisible by \\( n \\).\n\nConclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{ \\text{This proves that among any \\( n+1 \\) integers, there are two with a difference divisible by } n. }\n\\]\n\n### Question 2:\nWe need to show that for any \\( n \\), there exists a multiple of \\( n \\) that consists of at most \\( n \\) digits, all of which are either 0 or 1.\n\n1. Consider the sequence of numbers:\n   \\[\n   a_0 = 0, \\quad a_1 = 1, \\quad a_2 = 11, \\quad a_3 = 111, \\quad \\ldots, \\quad a_i = \\underbrace{111 \\ldots 1}_{i \\text{ ones}}\n   \\]\n\n2. Observe that \\( a_k \\) (where \\( k \\) ranges from 0 to \\( n \\)) is a number consisting of \\( k \\) digits, all set to 1. For instance:\n   \\[\n   a_1 = 1, \\quad a_2 = 11, \\quad a_3 = 111, \\quad \\ldots, \\quad a_i = \\sum_{j=0}^{i-1} 10^j\n   \\]\n\n3. Consider the remainders of these numbers when divided by \\( n \\):\n   \\[\n   r_0 = a_0 \\mod n, \\quad r_1 = a_1 \\mod n, \\quad \\ldots, \\quad r_n = a_n \\mod n\n   \\]\n\n4. By the pigeonhole principle, among these \\( n+1 \\) remainders, at least two must be the same since there are only \\( n \\) possible remainders.\n\n5. Suppose \\( r_i = r_j \\) where \\( i \\neq j \\). Then:\n   \\[\n   a_i \\equiv a_j \\pmod{n}\n   \\]\n\n6. The difference \\( a_j - a_i \\) would thus be a multiple of \\( n \\). Observe that \\( a_j \\) and \\( a_i \\) are composed of digits all equal to 1, hence \\( a_j - a_i \\) consists of digits all equal to 0 or 1.\n\n7. \\( a_j - a_i \\) will have at most \\( n \\) digits, as \\( j \\) and \\( i \\) are less than or equal to \\( n \\).\n\nConclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{ \\text{We have shown that for any } n, \\text { there exists a multiple of } n \\text{ consisting of at most } n \\text{ digits, all of which are either 0 or 1.} }\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "